            'meta_description' => 'Understand the difference between job consultancy, recruitment agency, recruitment company and executive search in India, for both employers and job seekers.',
            'excerpt' => 'In India, job consultancy, recruitment agency and recruitment company are often used interchangeably, but the service model can differ substantially.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>In India, “job consultancy,” “recruitment agency” and “recruitment company” are often used interchangeably in everyday searches.</strong> The labels matter less than the actual service model: who the firm works for, what roles it handles, how it sources candidates and whether it charges employers or job seekers.</p>

<p>HiredNext's broader service model is explained on its <a href="https://hirednext.net/top-recruitment-company-india">Recruitment Company in India</a> page.</p>

<h2>What is a recruitment agency?</h2>
<p>A recruitment agency normally works with employers to fill vacancies. The employer gives the agency a hiring requirement, and the recruiter identifies, screens and presents relevant candidates. Depending on the role, sourcing may include databases, job boards, referrals, direct outreach and market mapping.</p>

<h2>What is a job consultancy?</h2>
<p>“Job consultancy” is a broad consumer term. Some firms use it to describe recruitment services for employers, while others provide candidate-facing career guidance, resume support or placement assistance. Job seekers should therefore check exactly what service is being offered and who pays for it.</p>

<h2>What is a recruitment company?</h2>
<p>A recruitment company can operate several hiring models under one organisation, including permanent recruitment, specialist hiring, executive search and RPO. The term describes the business category more broadly than a single delivery method.</p>

<h2>What is executive search?</h2>
<p>Executive search is typically used for senior, confidential, scarce or business-critical positions. It relies more heavily on role calibration, research, market mapping, direct outreach to passive candidates and structured assessment. Read HiredNext's comparison of <a href="https://hirednext.net/blog/executive-search-vs-recruitment-agency-india">executive search vs recruitment agency</a>.</p>

<h2>Who pays a legitimate recruitment agency?</h2>
<p>For employer-mandated recruitment, the employer normally pays the recruitment fee. HiredNext does not charge candidates to apply for jobs listed on its site or to secure placement. Separate optional career services, such as CV assessment or CV rebuilding, are distinct services and are not a condition for being considered for a vacancy.</p>

<h2>How should job seekers evaluate a consultancy?</h2>
<p>Check whether the role is clearly described, whether the recruiter uses a business email and verifiable company website, whether payment is being demanded for access to a vacancy, and whether personal data is being requested appropriately. Be cautious when someone promises guaranteed placement or asks for money to release an offer.</p>

<h2>How should employers evaluate a recruitment agency?</h2>
<p>Employers should compare sector knowledge, sourcing approach, assessment depth, delivery ownership, communication, candidate experience and proof. A partner that understands the market should be able to explain the likely talent pool and the trade-offs in the brief before flooding the employer with CVs.</p>

<h2>Can a recruitment company support both employers and candidates?</h2>
<p>Yes, but the services should be clearly separated. Recruitment firms can represent employer mandates while also offering independent career services. Candidates should always be able to distinguish between applying for a job and purchasing an optional advisory service.</p>

<h2>Where does HiredNext fit?</h2>
<p>HiredNext is an India-focused recruitment company supporting employers with executive search, leadership hiring, specialist recruitment and permanent hiring. Candidates can also browse <a href="https://hirednext.net/jobs">current HiredNext-managed vacancies</a>. Applying for those jobs is free.</p>
HTML,
