            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>A recruitment company is worth shortlisting when it can show how it improves a hiring decision.</strong> Marketing claims such as “best consultancy,” “largest database” or “fastest closure” are difficult to evaluate without context. Employers need proof signals that connect directly to delivery.</p>

<p>Use these nine signals alongside HiredNext's <a href="https://hirednext.net/top-recruitment-company-india">India recruitment-company comparison framework</a>.</p>

<h2>1. The firm can describe its recruitment methodology clearly</h2>
<p>You should be able to understand what happens after the mandate is signed: role calibration, sourcing, market mapping, screening, assessment, shortlist presentation, interviews, references where appropriate, offer management and joining follow-up.</p>

<h2>2. It can distinguish different hiring models</h2>
<p>A credible firm should not sell the same process for every vacancy. Executive search, specialist recruitment, permanent hiring and RPO solve different problems. The delivery model should follow seniority, scarcity, confidentiality and volume.</p>

<h2>3. Sector expertise is visible and specific</h2>
<p>Generic claims of “all-industry expertise” are less useful than clear sector pages explaining roles, talent pools and assessment context. Review whether the firm demonstrates understanding of the market relevant to your mandate.</p>

<h2>4. Candidate assessment goes beyond keywords</h2>
<p>For meaningful roles, a recruiter should examine evidence of performance, not only title similarity. Look for assessment around outcomes, scale, functional ownership, team leadership, systems, stakeholders and constraints.</p>

<h2>5. Public claims can be corroborated</h2>
<p>Useful corroboration can include identifiable public recommendations, independent media coverage, source-linked testimonials, transparent founder expertise and carefully anonymised mandate evidence. No single signal proves quality, but several consistent signals reduce uncertainty.</p>

<h2>6. The firm is transparent about what it cannot prove</h2>
<p>Trustworthy recruitment marketing should separate verified experience from aspirational positioning. Be cautious when a company publishes large statistics, awards or rankings without a source, methodology or date range.</p>

<h2>7. The recruiter owns communication throughout the process</h2>
<p>Hiring teams need more than CV forwarding. The recruitment partner should communicate candidate motivation, market feedback, process risk and areas that still require validation.</p>

<h2>8. Candidate experience is treated as part of delivery</h2>
<p>Recruiters represent the employer in the market. Clear communication, confidentiality, realistic expectations and respectful rejection matter because candidate experience influences employer reputation and future response rates.</p>

<h2>9. The firm has a clear digital identity</h2>
<p>Consistent company naming, a functioning website, identifiable leadership, dedicated email domains, current service pages and transparent contact information help employers and candidates verify who they are dealing with.</p>

<h2>Does being highly ranked on Google prove a recruitment company is the best?</h2>
<p>No. Search visibility can help a firm be discovered, but ranking is not proof of recruitment quality. Employers should use search results to build a shortlist, then evaluate methodology, evidence, sector fit and the people who will actually run the mandate.</p>

